/**
\fn Sprite & pdg::Sprite::attachAnimationPhysicsPart(Part * part, Part * parent)
\brief Register a physical Part in a physical rig's mass assembly.

Adds the Part's existing mass to physics.getMass(). No other body's mass or
inertia changes. The Part must belong to this Sprite and already have a body.
An accessory must have no Part parent, bone/slot/socket binding, or mounted
Sprite. Its current control mode is retained. Skeletal Parts previously detached
from this rig can be registered again; they start Dynamic.

The optional parent records assembly hierarchy for subtree detachment. It does
not create a joint, move a body, bind artwork, or claim an animation drive.
Create joints explicitly with PhysicsBody. For skeletal Parts, the nearest
attached skeletal ancestor is automatic, and an explicit parent must match.
For accessories, an omitted or null parent means an independent assembly member.
Registering the same Part again is harmless; changing its parent requires detach.

Only registered bodies contribute to the total; arbitrary joint connectivity
never adds mass. Whole-rig animation modes and recovery select attached skeletal
bodies. An accessory keeps its own body control, responding through its joints.

\param part Physical Part owned by this Sprite.
\param parent Attached Part used for assembly hierarchy; optional, default null.
\return This Sprite, for chaining.

\note Requires an enabled rig from setupPhysicsFromAnimationRig() or
setupAnimationPhysics(), and a Chipmunk layer. Use transferPart() to move a foreign Part into this Sprite before registering it. Call outside animation modifiers and locked physics callbacks. Finish
or cancel active recovery first. Explicitly release manual/Part-IK drives before
reattaching skeletal bodies. Invalid edits throw before changing membership.
Membership edits cancel pending mode-completion notices. The example assumes a
rig with a hand Part and layer coordinates measured in centimeters.
\par Example
\code{.cpp}
auto* hand = sprite.findPart("hand");
auto* weight = sprite.createPart("weight");
auto& body = weight->setupPhysicsBody(10, 400); // kg and kg*cm^2
const auto grip = hand->physics.getState();
body.teleport(pdg::Point(grip.x, grip.y), grip.rotation);
weight->setupCollider().setBox(pdg::Rect(-10, -5, 10, 5));
sprite.attachAnimationPhysicsPart(weight, hand);
hand->physics.createPivotJoint(body);
hand->physics.createGear(body, 1, 0);
sprite.detachAnimationPhysicsPart(weight); // bodies remain Sprite-owned
\endcode

\see detachAnimationPhysicsPart(), isAnimationPhysicsPartAttached(), setupPhysicsFromAnimationRig()
*/

/**
\fn Sprite & pdg::Sprite::detachAnimationPhysicsPart(Part * part, bool includeDescendants)
\brief Release a Part or subtree from the physics assembly.

Removes the selected members' masses from the Sprite total without redistributing
any mass or inertia. Skeletal bodies become Dynamic and stop following animation;
artwork continues following their physical bones. Body identity, pose, velocity,
collider geometry and collision filters are retained. Joints within the selected
set remain intact; every joint crossing its boundary is disconnected, including
connections to external bodies. Retained disconnected constraint handles are
safe to inspect but report inactive.

Descendants follow assembly hierarchy, including registered accessories. With
includeDescendants false, surviving children are logically reparented to the
nearest surviving ancestor; no replacement physical joints are created.
Detached bodies are excluded from whole-rig control, recovery and aggregate
mass edits. Address them directly through their Part's physics object.

If the selected root leaves, the remaining attached skeletal bone named root is
preferred, otherwise the one with the most assembly descendants (ties use body
definition order). Whole-Sprite impulses and root follow use this replacement.
At least one skeletal body must remain; disableAnimationPhysics() tears down the
whole rig. Detaching a nonmember is a no-op.

This releases assembly membership, not Sprite ownership. Parts and bodies remain
alive while this Sprite/rig does. Disabling the rig removes all originally mapped
bodies, including detached ones. Ordinary accessory Parts retain their bodies
but stop contributing to the aggregate. Removing an accessory Part or its body
also removes its membership. Mapped Part removal still requires rig disable; transferPart() can instead release mapped bodies into another Sprite.

\param part Part owned by this Sprite.
\param includeDescendants Include the assembly subtree; optional, default true.
\return This Sprite, for chaining.

\note The same rig, callback and recovery restrictions as
attachAnimationPhysicsPart() apply. Release manual/Part-IK drives on selected
skeletal bodies first. Reattachment does not restore disconnected joints or snap
the piece into place; restore the desired pose/constraints explicitly.
\see attachAnimationPhysicsPart(), isAnimationPhysicsPartAttached(), disableAnimationPhysics()
*/

/**
\fn bool pdg::Sprite::isAnimationPhysicsPartAttached(const Part * part) const
\brief Check whether a Part contributes to this Sprite's physical assembly.
\param part Part to query.
\return True for a registered skeletal or accessory Part; false for a detached
Part, another Sprite's Part, or a disabled rig.
\see attachAnimationPhysicsPart(), detachAnimationPhysicsPart()
*/
